WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:00.967
Yeah.

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:01.735
It's something

00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:02.569
that the family is saying

00:00:02.569 --> 00:00:04.170
was a long time illness

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.638
that he'd been suffering from Aaron.

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:06.406
But still,

00:00:06.406 --> 00:00:08.575
when someone this iconic passes away,

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:10.243
you definitely see the reverberation

00:00:10.243 --> 00:00:11.511
and the feeling

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:13.413
going out that someone who just created

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.881
so much through all of his music

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.282
and the writing and his guitar

00:00:16.282 --> 00:00:17.550
playing as well.

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:18.151
Just feeling that.

00:00:18.151 --> 00:00:18.885
I want to read to you

00:00:18.885 --> 00:00:19.652
a bit

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:20.453
of the statement

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:21.955
from David Crosby's family

00:00:21.955 --> 00:00:23.757
that we have received here at CNN.

00:00:23.757 --> 00:00:24.924
It says in part,

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.226
It is with great sadness

00:00:26.226 --> 00:00:27.327
after a long illness

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.528
that our beloved David

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.563
Crosby, Crosby has passed away.

00:00:30.964 --> 00:00:33.266
He was lovingly surrounded by his wife

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:35.468
and soul mate Jan and son Jango.

00:00:36.002 --> 00:00:37.670
Although he is no longer here with us,

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:38.405
his humanity

00:00:38.405 --> 00:00:39.105
and kind soul

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:41.474
will continue to guide and inspire us.

00:00:41.775 --> 00:00:43.443
His legacy will continue to live on

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.211
through his legendary music.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.513
And they go on to wish peace, love

00:00:46.513 --> 00:00:46.946
and harmony

00:00:46.946 --> 00:00:47.881
to all

00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:48.548
who knew David

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:50.750
and to all the people that he touched.

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:52.152
And one of the people that he did

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:54.421
touch as well was Melissa Etheridge,

00:00:54.421 --> 00:00:55.321
who was his good friend.

00:00:55.321 --> 00:00:58.091
But also he was the biological father

00:00:58.291 --> 00:00:59.426
to her children.

00:00:59.426 --> 00:01:00.293
And let me just read

00:01:00.293 --> 00:01:02.462
what her tweet says here.

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:03.530
It says, I am a grieving.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:04.197
I am grieving

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:05.231
the loss of my friend

00:01:05.231 --> 00:01:07.233
and Bailey's biological father, David.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:09.302
He gave me the gift of family

00:01:09.302 --> 00:01:11.037
I will forever be grateful to him.

00:01:11.037 --> 00:01:12.439
Jango and Jan,

00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:13.773
his music and legacy

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.241
will inspire many generations

00:01:15.241 --> 00:01:17.343
to come a true treasure.

00:01:17.744 --> 00:01:19.145
So there you have people who know him

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:19.946
knew him

00:01:19.946 --> 00:01:22.215
not just for his music, but also knew

00:01:22.782 --> 00:01:24.084
David Crosby, the person

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:26.653
mourning the loss of this iconic, iconic

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:29.255
American musician. Aaron.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:30.290
All right, Stephanie.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.191
And as you point out,

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:32.992
iconic musician with a

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.161
with it with a complicated life

00:01:35.161 --> 00:01:37.864
that so many followed over so many years.

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:38.731
Thank you so much.

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:39.432
And let's bring in now

00:01:39.432 --> 00:01:41.067
Bill Weir, who interviewed Crosby

00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:42.368
for a special on Woodstock,

00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:43.803
and Jim Ray, the chief

00:01:43.803 --> 00:01:45.371
correspondent for Inside Edition.

00:01:45.371 --> 00:01:47.173
So thanks so much to both of you.

00:01:47.173 --> 00:01:48.141
Bill, you're here with me

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:48.942
let me start with that.

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.844
You traveled to David Crosby's home.

00:01:50.844 --> 00:01:51.478
You interviewed him

00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:52.545
extensively for this

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:53.680
documentary on Woodstock,

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:54.447
which would have been

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:55.148
sort of their

00:01:55.148 --> 00:01:57.851
their big moment, right on to the stage

00:01:57.851 --> 00:02:00.153
shall we say, for the first time.

00:02:00.153 --> 00:02:01.387
What was that like?

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:03.690
It was such a thrill on so many levels

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:05.225
as a music fan. It was a thrill.

00:02:05.225 --> 00:02:07.026
But he's one of my hands-down top

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:07.994
three interviews ever

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:10.130
because he's so open and honest.

00:02:10.330 --> 00:02:13.500
He could be a prickly S.O.B.,

00:02:13.766 --> 00:02:15.368
as many of his bandmates will attest,

00:02:15.368 --> 00:02:16.469
but he could be the sweetest

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:19.205
just loving guy.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:19.506
And when

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:20.273
you're sitting in his house,

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:21.241
he says you want to hear some music

00:02:21.241 --> 00:02:22.108
I've just written,

00:02:22.108 --> 00:02:23.409
and he's got his ponies there.

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:24.811
He lives in sort of Central Valley near

00:02:24.811 --> 00:02:26.279
wine country

00:02:26.279 --> 00:02:28.781
as cannabis plants his lovely wife, Jan,

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:29.883
and his family,

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:31.184
because his really theme later

00:02:31.184 --> 00:02:32.385
in life was second chances.

00:02:32.385 --> 00:02:32.919
He would joke that

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:34.320
I can't believe Hendrix

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:35.488
and Morrison didn't make it.

00:02:35.488 --> 00:02:36.356
And I'm still here

00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:37.590
because of the way he

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:38.958
punished himself physically.

00:02:38.958 --> 00:02:40.393
He did some time for his cocaine

00:02:40.393 --> 00:02:41.327
and heroin addiction

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:42.795
in Texas in the eighties,

00:02:42.795 --> 00:02:44.364
but then and had a liver transplant,

00:02:44.364 --> 00:02:45.131
but then came back

00:02:45.131 --> 00:02:47.100
and wasn't content to just do cover songs

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.468
and he wrote five albums

00:02:48.468 --> 00:02:49.202
and five years

00:02:49.202 --> 00:02:49.669
later in life,

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.871
even as his health was failing.

00:02:51.871 --> 00:02:53.673
And a lot of complicated

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.141
feelings out there.

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:55.742
Absolutely.

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:57.377
But but an incredible person

00:02:57.377 --> 00:02:58.278
with so many layers

00:02:58.278 --> 00:02:59.612
and why it was so compelling.

00:02:59.612 --> 00:03:00.446
I mean, Jim,

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:01.314
we mentioned Crosby, Stills

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:01.981
and Nash perform

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:02.749
just their second gig

00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:03.483
at Woodstock, which was,

00:03:03.483 --> 00:03:04.217
of course,

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:05.685
why why Bill was speaking to him

00:03:05.685 --> 00:03:06.986
originally

00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:08.922
when he spoke to Larry King about this.

00:03:08.922 --> 00:03:10.256
He was reflecting on the 25th

00:03:10.256 --> 00:03:11.491
anniversary of Woodstock,

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:13.092
and that was back in 1994.

00:03:13.092 --> 00:03:14.160
I just wanted to play this one

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:16.763
particular thing he said to cause you

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.532
like the rest of our generation,

00:03:19.532 --> 00:03:20.233
you know,

00:03:21.201 --> 00:03:23.369
we had a rough road, you know,

00:03:23.369 --> 00:03:26.272
we went through some very tough stuff.

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:27.373
I'm one of the lucky ones

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:29.976
that wound up alive afterwards.

00:03:29.976 --> 00:03:32.011
I feel now

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.346
incredibly grateful

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:36.282
to get to play music and

00:03:36.282 --> 00:03:37.984
be a part of this.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:40.587
I'm really awfully happy

00:03:40.687 --> 00:03:42.722
I had what the French called Rage one.

00:03:42.722 --> 00:03:43.256
That's great.

00:03:43.256 --> 00:03:45.291
I, I have a reason to be here.

00:03:45.291 --> 00:03:46.893
I'm a, I'm lucky in that

00:03:46.893 --> 00:03:49.262
I really love to play music

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:52.565
and I've got great people to do on with

00:03:53.933 --> 00:03:55.401
just how big of a loss

00:03:55.401 --> 00:03:57.804
is this of someone who was such an icon

00:03:57.804 --> 00:03:59.839
and over so many decades?

00:04:01.808 --> 00:04:02.342
First of all,

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:03.810
I envy Bill Weir

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:05.011
for having the opportunity

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.412
to talk with him in person.

00:04:06.412 --> 00:04:08.514
He had a social responsibility

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.984
that he showed through his music

00:04:11.251 --> 00:04:13.219
and his music reflected the times

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:15.021
and the strife that he talked about.

00:04:15.021 --> 00:04:16.022
I became a teenager.

00:04:16.022 --> 00:04:17.790
I became 13 in 1969

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.259
when they played Woodstock

00:04:19.259 --> 00:04:20.727
and had a tremendous influence

00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:22.829
on me and others. In my generation.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:24.497
I think that you

00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:26.099
will remember David Crosby,

00:04:26.099 --> 00:04:27.200
not just for his music,

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:28.668
but for the beautiful harmonies

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.103
that he and Crosby,

00:04:30.103 --> 00:04:31.471
Stills, Stills and Young

00:04:31.471 --> 00:04:33.172
would perform together.

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:34.774
But as much of harmony

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:36.776
as they would have vocally,

00:04:36.776 --> 00:04:38.978
there was a tremendous amount of strife

00:04:38.978 --> 00:04:40.480
personally with his bandmates,

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:41.714
and he had falling out with them.

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:42.448
But, but,

00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.851
but the legacy of his music will endure.

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:45.718
And I think it's

00:04:45.718 --> 00:04:46.953
I think it's a beautiful

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:48.254
and long lasting one

00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:49.389
and perhaps incredible

00:04:49.389 --> 00:04:51.090
when you think about to have such strife

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.126
and sort of the band name keeps changing.

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:54.127
People are in and out yet

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:55.228
to be able to create something

00:04:55.228 --> 00:04:56.462
so beautiful,

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:58.431
something we all can just ponder.

00:04:58.431 --> 00:04:58.831
And Bill,

00:04:58.831 --> 00:04:59.699
since you did have a chance

00:04:59.699 --> 00:05:00.300
to talk to him,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:02.235
I think what's amazing is among

00:05:02.235 --> 00:05:03.603
the things you talked about with him

00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:04.504
was death,

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.606
death and legacy at a point in his life

00:05:06.606 --> 00:05:08.041
when he was able to reflect upon that.

00:05:08.041 --> 00:05:09.275
And I wanted to play part of the exchange

00:05:09.275 --> 00:05:10.610
he had with you and to see that

00:05:12.211 --> 00:05:13.046
in this burst of

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:14.814
creativity that you've had, you

00:05:14.814 --> 00:05:15.748
sing about death

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:17.684
and to stand up and face.

00:05:17.684 --> 00:05:18.451
Do you think about how

00:05:18.451 --> 00:05:20.119
you want to be remembered?

00:05:20.219 --> 00:05:21.354
Not so much

00:05:22.655 --> 00:05:24.524
The songs will do that.

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.559
They're the best I can do.

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:27.360
Just a weird thing.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:29.162
Everybody's scared to talk about it.

00:05:29.162 --> 00:05:29.662
The question is,

00:05:29.662 --> 00:05:31.998
what are you going to do? With it?

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:34.000
How do you spend that two weeks

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:35.635
or that ten years?

00:05:35.635 --> 00:05:37.470
And I got that figured out.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.772
Family music.

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:46.179
Yeah, we got to get ourselves back to God

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:49.282
because it's the only thing I can do

00:05:49.982 --> 00:05:50.817
It's a goosebumps.

00:05:50.817 --> 00:05:53.119
He gives me that voice, gives me goose.

00:05:53.319 --> 00:05:54.520
Especially with Joni Mitchell

00:05:54.520 --> 00:05:55.355
in there, who they were.

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:56.989
They were a pair for a little while,

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:58.091
an item for a while.

00:05:58.091 --> 00:05:59.225
And then Graham Nash was in

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:00.460
that made part of the complication

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:01.127
right there.

00:06:01.127 --> 00:06:01.861
But yeah,

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:02.862
later in life,

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:03.062
you know,

00:06:03.062 --> 00:06:03.863
he gave up a son

00:06:03.863 --> 00:06:05.531
for adoption in the sixties

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:06.165
named Raymond,

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:08.368
who came back into his life later,

00:06:08.368 --> 00:06:09.369
who became a musician.

00:06:09.369 --> 00:06:11.371
And they record it together.

00:06:11.371 --> 00:06:12.672
He just had such sweet.

00:06:12.672 --> 00:06:14.340
But again, to Jim's point,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:15.975
it struck me when we talked together,

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:17.610
like if the guys who made that three part

00:06:17.610 --> 00:06:19.178
harmony at three in the morning

00:06:19.178 --> 00:06:21.214
at Woodstock can't keep it together,

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:22.849
what hope is there for the rest of us?

00:06:22.849 --> 00:06:24.083
And he said, no,

00:06:24.083 --> 00:06:25.251
no, there was bad stuff

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:25.952
before that happened.

00:06:25.952 --> 00:06:26.419
Bad stuff

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.388
after Woodstock was what we could be.

00:06:28.388 --> 00:06:30.523
We should strive towards that.

00:06:30.723 --> 00:06:32.792
It's also amazing in what he said here.

00:06:32.792 --> 00:06:33.192
And, you know,

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:34.827
maybe it's at the stage of life

00:06:34.827 --> 00:06:35.461
that I'm at.

00:06:35.461 --> 00:06:36.796
You think about your life.

00:06:36.796 --> 00:06:38.131
And he says,

00:06:38.131 --> 00:06:39.065
How do you spend those

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:40.066
two weeks or ten years?

00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:41.901
And I got that figured out.

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:43.169
There's something very beautiful

00:06:43.169 --> 00:06:44.637
about that. Absolutely.

00:06:44.637 --> 00:06:45.738
I think, you know,

00:06:45.738 --> 00:06:48.674
he would say I've had a lucky long run.

00:06:48.674 --> 00:06:50.710
I think you could throw a party for me.

00:06:51.544 --> 00:06:52.745
But the music he made, like

00:06:52.745 --> 00:06:54.113
he said, especially the new stuff,

00:06:54.113 --> 00:06:54.647
hopefully go

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:55.214
people go back

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:56.716
and listen to some of the newer stuff

00:06:56.716 --> 00:06:58.351
and honor him. That way.

00:06:58.351 --> 00:06:59.952
Jim Graham Nash, who Crosby,

00:06:59.952 --> 00:07:01.788
of course, had a falling out with.

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.723
He just reacted.

00:07:03.723 --> 00:07:05.124
Talking of quote him, it's

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:05.992
with a deep and profound

00:07:05.992 --> 00:07:07.360
sadness that I learned my friend

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.195
David Crosby has passed.

00:07:09.195 --> 00:07:11.497
I know people tend to reflect

00:07:11.497 --> 00:07:12.665
tend to focus on how volatile

00:07:12.665 --> 00:07:14.033
our relationship has been at times.

00:07:14.033 --> 00:07:15.468
But what has always mattered to David

00:07:15.468 --> 00:07:16.335
and me more than anything

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:16.836
was the pure

00:07:16.836 --> 00:07:18.805
joy of the music we created together,

00:07:18.805 --> 00:07:20.673
the sound we discovered with one another,

00:07:20.673 --> 00:07:21.474
and the deep friendship

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:23.576
we shared over all these many long years.

00:07:23.576 --> 00:07:25.445
David was fearless in life

00:07:25.445 --> 00:07:27.780
and in music that is a tribute

00:07:30.183 --> 00:07:30.783
it is.

00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:32.351
And I think that

00:07:32.351 --> 00:07:33.419
his friends loved him

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:34.387
and often hated him,

00:07:34.387 --> 00:07:35.755
but they always had the music

00:07:35.755 --> 00:07:37.323
and that was what lasted.

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:39.792
And it kept them bonded together

00:07:39.792 --> 00:07:42.061
and will endure long after they're gone.

00:07:42.061 --> 00:07:43.729
And I think it's still beautiful today.

